WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:12.410
In this lesson, we're going to discuss a healthcare
application of clustering called phenotyping.

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.590
Phenotypes are medical concepts such as diseases
or conditions.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:21.360
We know many phenotypes of patients based
on existing medical knowledge such as major

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:22.620
diseases.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.400
But there are many more phenotypes and their
subtypes out there that we haven't discovered.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:33.050
Computational phenotyping is a way to use
data available to us to discover those novel

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:34.750
phenotypes.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.920
Phenotypes aren't just for disease diagnosis,
though.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.100
We can also use those phenotypes for predicting
healthcare cost, readmission risk, and supporting

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.719
genomic studies.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:50.059
Now let's talk about computational phenotyping.

00:00:50.059 --> 00:00:54.329
Computational phenotyping is about converting
raw electronic health record through phenotyping

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:59.360
algorithms into a set of meaningful medical
concepts, or phenotypes.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.379
For example, a specific disease can be a phenotype.

00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:05.980
Such as type 2 diabetes.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.659
And the raw data, in this case, consists of
many different sources.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:16.200
Such as demographics about patients, diagnosis
code, medication information, clinical procedure,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.040
lab result, and clinical notes.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.740
There are many reasons why phenotypes are
not represented consistently or reliably in

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:24.939
the raw data.

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:30.799
First, the data are noisy, there are missing
data and raw information in the raw data.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:37.039
And second, the main usage of this data is
to support clinical operations, such as billing.

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:40.170
And it's not designed directly for supporting
research.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.710
Third, there are many overlapping and redundant
information.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:49.149
For example, diagnosis information can be
found in the structure field corresponding

00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:51.380
to diagnosis code.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.619
But the same information can also be present
as end structure information in the clinical

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:57.039
notes.

00:01:57.039 --> 00:02:00.770
This information is overlapping and redundant
in the raw data.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:06.259
And phenotyping is this process of deriving
research grade phenotypes from clinical data,

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.490
using computer algorithms.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:13.630
Here's a phenotyping algorithm for Type 2
diabetes.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:18.140
And the goal here is, we want to determine
whether patient has Type 2 diabetes based

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.880
on her electronic health records.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.660
For example, we can first check whether the
patient has Type 1 diabetes code in her record.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:32.530
If no, then we check whether Type 2 diabetes
diagnosis are present in her record.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.590
If still no, then we check medication for
Type 2 diabetes.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:41.330
Then, check abnormal lab result related to
diabetes.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:46.270
If these two steps are confirmed then we confirm
she has Type 2 diabetes.

00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:50.940
There are many different paths that can lead
to the confirmation of Type 2 diabetes, and

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.560
this decision flow is a phenotyping algorithm.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.700
This was developed manually by clinical experts.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.760
There are many different applications that
require phenotyping.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:07.590
For example, genomic study, which is about
finding relationship between genomic data

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.190
and phenotypic data.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:14.730
Clinical predictive modeling, which is about
building an accurate, robust, and interpretable

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:20.810
prediction model about disease onset and other
related targets, such as hospitalization.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:25.120
And pragmatic clinical trials, which is about
comparing treatment effectiveness in the real

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.840
world clinical environment using observational
data, like electronic health records.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.760
And healthcare quality measurement, which
is about measuring efficiency and quality

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.650
of care across different hospitals.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.860
All those applications depends on phenotyping
algorithms.

